From 409087b94cb32c01b1cbb4c5b9f6dc3a568a39fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Eaton <weaton@redhat.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 12:23:51 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] [Distributed] Recreate NCCL communicators across a process
 checkpoint

/checkpoint/prepare left the NCCL communicator alive, so each TP worker
kept its peers' buffers mapped when the process was checkpointed. On driver
580, cuda-checkpoint then refuses to restore the workers, and suspending the
communicator with ncclCommSuspend only helps when the restore lands on the
same GPUs: after a remap, ncclCommResume leaves peer mappings that fault,
and a new cuMem communicator fails its first all-reduce.

checkpoint_prepare now destroys the communicator and checkpoint_restore
creates a new one from a fresh unique id broadcast over the CPU group.
CUDA graphs replay the old communicator and ncclCommDestroy waits for them,
so the worker releases its graphs before preparing (V2 model runner only)
and captures them again when the KV cache wakes, before scheduling resumes.

With custom all-reduce off, FlashInfer all-reduce off and
NCCL_CUMEM_ENABLE=0, a TP=2 CRIU dump and restore onto two other GPUs
returns the same greedy completion, eager and with CUDA graphs. Recapturing
the graphs adds about 7 s to the restore.
